---
// Shared détail-page scroll-reveal hero (quick-260724-mjp, generalized from
// quick-260724-l5i's édition-only hero component; sketch 005/Synthesis
// "Bold + Facts"): on desktop page scroll the lead photo pins
// (position: sticky) and shrinks from full-bleed to ~55% width settling
// left as a framed portrait; the space that opens on the right fills with
// the title in large Unbounded display type, then — when a format-details
// line is supplied — the pink-underlined format line crystallizes right
// after. A dependency-free vanilla-JS island (no client:* directive),
// mirroring Lightbox.astro's and HomeCarousel.astro's typed-Props /
// scoped-CSS / plain-module-<script> conventions.
//
// Used by both édition détail pages (formatText + caption + total) and
// gallery détail pages (no formatText/caption). quick-260724-uf5: the
// gallery hero previously accepted a no-crop opt-in prop (quick-260724-mjp)
// so the photographer's framing was never cropped, but per an explicit user
// reversal after seeing it live ("je veux pas de flou, je veux que la photo
// aie le meme format que sur la homepage") the hero was changed to ALWAYS
// render object-fit: cover on both éditions and galleries, matching the
// homepage carousel/grid crop exactly, and the opt-in prop was removed
// entirely rather than kept as speculative flexibility.
//
// quick-260803-bvu (Item 4) / quick-260803-jwl: `editionVariant` (see Props
// below) now scopes exactly ONE thing — suppressing the shared
// cross-document `hero-photo` view-transition name for éditions at desktop
// widths, fixing a real édition-to-édition navigation shake. Item 4's
// sibling, Item 7 (briefly rendering the édition hero uncropped, at its
// natural aspect ratio, diverging from the gallery hero's crop), was
// reverted by quick-260803-jwl at the owner's explicit request
// ("remets la primary en zoomée comme avant") — édition and gallery hero
// photos are rendered identically again, both cropping via the base
// `.detail-hero__img` rule below. Both édition [slug].astro twins still
// pass `editionVariant`; galleries never do — see
// tests/e2e/gallery.spec.ts's hero-photo desktop/mobile assertions, which
// must keep passing byte-unchanged.
//
// quick-260724-rhq: the reveal panel also accepts an optional real (i.e.
// non-decorative, non-aria-hidden) `statement` paragraph, rendered after the
// title and before the format line — the single accessible instance of the
// description once callers stop rendering it below the hero. Because the
// panel itself defaults to opacity:0 pending the scroll-reveal script, a
// <noscript> fallback forces `.detail-hero__reveal` to opacity:1/
// transform:none so the statement (and the rest of the panel) is reachable
// without JS, mirroring the exact pattern in GalleryGrid.astro.
//
// This component receives only pre-computed, already-localized primitives
// as props — all locale/image logic stays in the calling page's
// frontmatter. It never imports src/lib/sanity or src/lib/image, so no read
// token reaches the browser (mirrors Lightbox/HomeCarousel's threat-model
// boundary).
//
// `total` is accepted for interface symmetry with édition calling pages
// (which already bake it into `heroAriaLabel` before passing it down) but
// is not otherwise consumed here — the aria-label is the single source of
// truth for the "1 of N" text. It is optional since gallery calling pages
// don't have an equivalent second-stage fact to show.
interface Props {
  leadPhotoSrc: string;
  leadPhotoSrcSet: string;
  leadPhotoAlt: string;
  title: string;
  heroAriaLabel: string;
  formatText?: string;
  caption?: string;
  statement?: string;
  total?: number;
  // quick-260724-oep: which Lightbox slide this hero trigger opens. Editions
  // (and any caller that omits this prop) keep the historical index-0
  // trigger via this default — byte-identical output. Gallery detail pages
  // pass the real index of their landscape-preferred hero image.
  heroIndex?: number;
  // quick-260724-wdr: locale-aware text label rendered above the scroll-down
  // chevron ("Faire défiler" / "Scroll"), strengthening the affordance that
  // live user testing found unclear. Required (not optional/defaulted) so
  // `astro check` fails the build if any of the four detail-page twins is
  // missed — this component never imports src/lib/sanity, so it cannot
  // localize the label itself; the calling page must supply it.
  scrollHintLabel: string;
  // quick-260725-tqs (Item 6): optional — its PRESENCE is the scroll-up-to-
  // return gesture's on/off switch (opt-in scoping). Originally gallery-
  // only; quick-260803-bvu (Item 6) also threads this through from édition
  // detail pages, passing their `overviewHref` (the éditions list) instead
  // of a gallery's `?carousel=<slug>` homepage URL — same gesture, same
  // prop, two different destinations depending on which caller supplies
  // it. Kept as `carouselReturnHref` rather than renamed (would be a
  // slightly inaccurate name for the édition case) per
  // <scope_boundaries> — deliberately deferred, zero user-visible benefit
  // for the churn it would cause across GalleryDetailBody.astro and both
  // gallery [slug].astro twins.
  carouselReturnHref?: string;
  // quick-260803-bvu (Item 4) / quick-260803-jwl: single opt-in scoping flag
  // controlling ONLY whether the shared cross-document `hero-photo`
  // view-transition name is suppressed at desktop widths (see the
  // file-header comment above). Defaults to false — today's gallery
  // behavior of keeping that shared name at desktop. Both édition heroes
  // and gallery heroes crop their photo the same way regardless of this
  // flag; it is no longer a per-caller behavior at all. Both édition
  // [slug].astro twins pass true; gallery pages never set it.
  editionVariant?: boolean;
  // Gallery detail pages use a separate editorial panel on phones. Editions
  // keep the established shared detail hero treatment.
  galleryVariant?: boolean;
  // Édition details use the same phone-only photo-then-panel structure as
  // galleries, but keep their neutral editorial panel rather than borrowing
  // a gallery accent colour.
  editionPanel?: boolean;
  accent?: string;
  accentText?: string;
  // The edition detail panel remains neutral on phones, but uses the ink
  // canvas on desktop where the scroll reveal opens a large side panel.
  // Gallery callers omit these props, so their desktop canvas remains their
  // own computed accent colour.
  desktopAccent?: string;
  desktopAccentText?: string;
}

const {
  leadPhotoSrc,
  leadPhotoSrcSet,
  leadPhotoAlt,
  title,
  formatText,
  heroAriaLabel,
  caption,
  statement,
  heroIndex = 0,
  scrollHintLabel,
  carouselReturnHref,
  editionVariant = false,
  galleryVariant = false,
  editionPanel = false,
  accent = '#A6FD29',
  accentText = '#1A1A1A',
  desktopAccent = accent,
  desktopAccentText = accentText,
} = Astro.props;
---

<div
  class={`detail-hero${editionVariant ? ' detail-hero--edition' : ''}${galleryVariant ? ' detail-hero--gallery' : ''}${editionPanel ? ' detail-hero--edition-panel' : ''}`}
  data-carousel-return-href={carouselReturnHref}
  style={{
    '--detail-hero-accent': accent,
    '--detail-hero-accent-text': accentText,
    '--detail-hero-desktop-accent': desktopAccent,
    '--detail-hero-desktop-accent-text': desktopAccentText,
  }}
>
  <div class="detail-hero__pin">
    <div class="detail-hero__photo">
      <button
        type="button"
        class="detail-hero__trigger"
        data-gallery-thumb
        data-index={heroIndex}
        aria-label={heroAriaLabel}
      >
        <img
          src={leadPhotoSrc}
          srcset={leadPhotoSrcSet}
          sizes="100vw"
          alt={leadPhotoAlt}
          class="detail-hero__img"
          loading="eager"
          decoding="async"
        />
      </button>
      <div class="detail-hero__scrim" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <p class="detail-hero__overlay-title" aria-hidden="true">{title}</p>
      <svg
        class="detail-hero__expand-icon"
        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
        width="24"
        height="24"
        fill="none"
        stroke="currentColor"
        stroke-width="1.8"
        aria-hidden="true"
      >
        <polyline points="9 3 3 3 3 9"></polyline>
        <polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline>
        <polyline points="21 15 21 21 15 21"></polyline>
        <polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <div class="detail-hero__reveal">
      {caption && <p class="detail-hero__caption" aria-hidden="true">{caption}</p>}
      <h1 class="detail-hero__reveal-title">{title}</h1>
      {statement && <p class="detail-hero__statement">{statement}</p>}
      {/* quick-260803-bvu (Item 5): this reveal-panel copy is now the ONLY
          instance of the format-details line — EditionDetailBody.astro no
          longer renders a second `.edition-detail__format` paragraph below
          the hero, so this is real content, not a decorative duplicate.
          aria-hidden removed accordingly so screen-reader users still get
          it (safe for galleries: they never pass formatText, so this
          element doesn't render there at all). */}
      {formatText && <span class="detail-hero__format">{formatText}</span>}
    </div>
    <div class="detail-hero__scroll-hint" aria-hidden="true">
      <span class="detail-hero__scroll-hint-label">{scrollHintLabel}</span>
      <svg
        class="detail-hero__scroll-hint-icon"
        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
        width="34"
        height="34"
        fill="none"
        stroke="white"
        stroke-width="2.25"
      >
        <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline>
      </svg>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<noscript>
  <style is:inline>
    .detail-hero__reveal {
      opacity: 1 !important;
      transform: none !important;
    }
  </style>
</noscript>

<script>
  // Proven Synthesis mechanism ported verbatim from
  // .planning/sketches/005-edition-hero-scroll-reveal/index.html
  // (onProgress ~lines 387-403), adapted from the sketch's bounded-div
  // scroll to real page scroll (progress derived from the track's own
  // getBoundingClientRect instead of a scoped viewport's scrollTop).
  function clamp01(v: number): number {
    return Math.max(0, Math.min(1, v));
  }

  function lerp(a: number, b: number, t: number): number {
    return a + (b - a) * t;
  }

  const track = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.detail-hero');
  const photo = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.detail-hero__photo');
  const scrim = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.detail-hero__scrim');
  const overlayTitle = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.detail-hero__overlay-title');
  const reveal = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.detail-hero__reveal');
  const revealFormat = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.detail-hero__format');
  const scrollHint = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.detail-hero__scroll-hint');

  // Scroll-down hint fade (quick-260724-uf5): a self-contained block, kept
  // OUTSIDE the reveal-driver guard below so it never depends on the
  // reveal's own element set being complete. Writing opacity on a
  // `display: none` element (mobile / reduced-motion desktop, per the CSS
  // below) is a harmless no-op, so no extra branching is needed here.
  if (scrollHint) {
    window.addEventListener(
      'scroll',
      () => {
        scrollHint.style.opacity = String(Math.max(0, 1 - window.scrollY / 150) * 0.85);
      },
      { passive: true },
    );
  }

  if (track && photo && scrim && overlayTitle && reveal) {
    const REVEAL_DISTANCE = 900;

    function onProgress(t: number) {
      const width = lerp(100, 55, t);
      photo!.style.left = '0%';
      photo!.style.right = 100 - width + '%';
      photo!.style.top = '0%';
      photo!.style.bottom = '0%';
      scrim!.style.opacity = String(clamp01(1 - t * 3.3));
      overlayTitle!.style.opacity = String(clamp01(1 - t * 4));
      const titleT = clamp01((t - 0.3) / 0.3);
      const formatT = clamp01((t - 0.6) / 0.4);
      // titleT ramps t=0.3->0.6 and formatT ramps t=0.6->1.0, so formatT
      // never exceeds titleT — using titleT alone here is mathematically
      // identical to the previous Math.max(titleT, formatT): the reveal
      // panel already reached full opacity by the time the title finished.
      reveal!.style.opacity = String(titleT);
      reveal!.style.transform = `translateY(-50%) translateX(${lerp(24, 0, titleT)}px)`;
      if (revealFormat) {
        revealFormat.style.opacity = String(formatT);
        revealFormat.style.transform = `translateY(${lerp(8, 0, formatT)}px)`;
      }
    }

    function clearInlineStyles() {
      // CSS end-state (reduced-motion / mobile media queries) must fully
      // control layout when the driver is inactive — inline styles set by
      // a previous run of onProgress() would otherwise beat those
      // media-query rules.
      photo!.style.removeProperty('left');
      photo!.style.removeProperty('right');
      photo!.style.removeProperty('top');
      photo!.style.removeProperty('bottom');
      scrim!.style.removeProperty('opacity');
      overlayTitle!.style.removeProperty('opacity');
      reveal!.style.removeProperty('opacity');
      reveal!.style.removeProperty('transform');
      if (revealFormat) {
        revealFormat.style.removeProperty('opacity');
        revealFormat.style.removeProperty('transform');
      }
    }

    const reduceMotion = matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');
    const desktop = matchMedia('(min-width: 768px)');

    let rafId: number | null = null;
    let scrollAttached = false;

    function computeProgress(): number {
      return clamp01(-track!.getBoundingClientRect().top / REVEAL_DISTANCE);
    }

    function onScroll() {
      if (rafId !== null) return;
      rafId = requestAnimationFrame(() => {
        rafId = null;
        onProgress(computeProgress());
      });
    }

    function setup() {
      if (reduceMotion.matches || !desktop.matches) {
        if (scrollAttached) {
          window.removeEventListener('scroll', onScroll);
          scrollAttached = false;
        }
        clearInlineStyles();
      } else {
        if (!scrollAttached) {
          window.addEventListener('scroll', onScroll, { passive: true });
          scrollAttached = true;
        }
        onProgress(computeProgress());
      }
    }

    setup();
    reduceMotion.addEventListener('change', setup);
    desktop.addEventListener('change', setup);

    let resizeTimer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null = null;
    window.addEventListener('resize', () => {
      if (resizeTimer) clearTimeout(resizeTimer);
      resizeTimer = setTimeout(setup, 100);
    });
  }
</script>

<script>
  // quick-260725-tqs (Item 6): the mirror image of the homepage carousel's
  // own scroll-to-open gesture (HomeCarousel.astro's registerDownwardIntent/
  // atBottom() accumulator) — a sustained upward push while genuinely at
  // the TOP of the page navigates back to wherever `carouselReturnHref`
  // points. Originally gallery-only (returns to the homepage carousel
  // showing the SAME gallery); quick-260803-bvu (Item 6) also activates it
  // for édition detail pages, which pass their `overviewHref` (the
  // éditions list) instead — same gesture, same engage/reset/threshold
  // logic below, two different destinations depending on which caller
  // supplied the prop (see the Props doc above). A NEW, independent
  // top-level script — deliberately NOT nested inside the reveal-driver
  // `if (track && photo && scrim && overlayTitle && reveal)` guard above,
  // so the gesture also works on mobile, where the reveal driver is
  // inactive.
  const track = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.detail-hero');
  const returnHref = track?.dataset.carouselReturnHref;

  if (returnHref) {
    // SAFETY DESIGN (documented per plan, mirrors the quick-260725-sj4
    // post-mortem in reverse): "at the top" (scrollY ~= 0) is trivially
    // true on EVERY fresh page load, so an accumulator armed from load
    // would misfire on ordinary small upward scroll corrections — the
    // mirror image of the quick-260725-sj4 bug (where "at the bottom" was
    // vacuously true at scrollY 0 because the footer had been hidden). The
    // `hasEngaged` gate is the fix: the gesture stays DISARMED until the
    // visitor has genuinely scrolled DOWN past ENGAGE_DISTANCE (300px — far
    // beyond a small re-read correction, well within the desktop reveal's
    // own 900px track, and reachable on mobile whenever a grid exists below
    // the hero) at least once; only then, AND only once back at the very
    // top, does a sustained >= RETURN_OVERSCROLL_THRESHOLD upward push
    // navigate. On a fresh load hasEngaged is false, so no upward wheel/
    // touch does anything. A tiny scroll-down-then-correct-up (< 300px)
    // never arms it. If a short page (e.g. a single-image gallery on
    // mobile with no grid) can't scroll 300px, the gesture simply never
    // arms — safe graceful degradation, no misfire. Listeners are passive
    // and never preventDefault, so normal scrolling, the lightbox trigger,
    // the scroll-reveal, and the scroll-hint above are all unaffected.
    const RETURN_OVERSCROLL_THRESHOLD = 150;
    const TOP_EPSILON = 4;
    const RESET_IDLE_MS = 800;
    const ENGAGE_DISTANCE = 300;

    let hasEngaged = false;
    let upAccum = 0;
    let lastUpTs = 0;
    let navigatingHome = false;

    function atTop(): boolean {
      return window.scrollY <= TOP_EPSILON;
    }

    // Engagement + reset scroll listener (passive): arms the gate once the
    // visitor has genuinely scrolled down, and resets any in-flight
    // accumulation the moment they leave the top — for ANY reason (mirrors
    // quick-260725-cfm's own stale-accumulator-reset fix for keyboard/
    // scrollbar/scrollTo paths, not just wheel/touch).
    window.addEventListener(
      'scroll',
      () => {
        if (window.scrollY >= ENGAGE_DISTANCE) hasEngaged = true;
        if (!atTop()) upAccum = 0;
      },
      { passive: true },
    );

    function navigateHome() {
      if (navigatingHome) return;
      navigatingHome = true;
      window.location.href = returnHref!;
    }

    // delta > 0 means upward scroll intent (i.e. the visitor is pushing the
    // page content downward / scrolling toward the top).
    function registerUpwardIntent(delta: number) {
      if (navigatingHome || !hasEngaged || !atTop()) return;
      if (delta <= 0) {
        upAccum = 0;
        return;
      }
      const now = Date.now();
      if (now - lastUpTs > RESET_IDLE_MS) {
        upAccum = 0;
      }
      upAccum += delta;
      lastUpTs = now;
      if (upAccum >= RETURN_OVERSCROLL_THRESHOLD) {
        navigateHome();
      }
    }

    // Desktop input: wheel deltaY < 0 = scrolling up = positive upward
    // intent. Never preventDefault.
    window.addEventListener(
      'wheel',
      (event) => {
        registerUpwardIntent(-event.deltaY);
      },
      { passive: true },
    );

    // Mobile input: an independent window-level touch accumulator mirroring
    // HomeCarousel's own downward-intent one, but for UPWARD intent — the
    // finger moving DOWN the screen means the content (and therefore the
    // scroll position) is moving UP.
    let lastMoveY = 0;
    let startX = 0;
    let startY = 0;
    window.addEventListener(
      'touchstart',
      (event) => {
        const touch = event.touches[0];
        if (!touch) return;
        lastMoveY = touch.clientY;
        startX = touch.clientX;
        startY = touch.clientY;
      },
      { passive: true },
    );
    window.addEventListener(
      'touchmove',
      (event) => {
        const touch = event.touches[0];
        if (!touch) return;
        const dy = touch.clientY - lastMoveY;
        if (Math.abs(touch.clientY - startY) > Math.abs(touch.clientX - startX)) {
          registerUpwardIntent(dy);
        }
        lastMoveY = touch.clientY;
      },
      { passive: true },
    );
  }
</script>

<style>
  .detail-hero {
    position: relative;
    height: calc(100svh + 900px);
    /* A deliberate colour canvas is visible while the remote Sanity image
       decodes. It replaces the old black flash on a slow mobile network. */
    background: var(--detail-hero-desktop-accent);
  }

  .detail-hero__pin {
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    height: 100svh;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--detail-hero-desktop-accent);
  }

  .detail-hero__photo {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .detail-hero__trigger {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    padding: 0;
    border: none;
    background: transparent;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .detail-hero__trigger:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--color-accent);
    outline-offset: var(--focus-ring-offset);
  }

  .detail-hero__img {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    transition: transform 0.2s ease;
  }

  .detail-hero__trigger:hover .detail-hero__img,
  .detail-hero__trigger:focus-visible .detail-hero__img {
    transform: scale(1.02);
  }

  /* Cross-document View Transition destination name (quick-260724-uf5,
     sketch 006) — DESKTOP ONLY, NEVER unconditional / NEVER on mobile. This
     is a direct precaution against the HOME-06/D-10/D-12 bug class: a
     permanent, always-on view-transition-name on a 100svh-sized element was
     the suspected mobile regression there, and `.detail-hero__img`/
     `.detail-hero__pin` are likewise 100svh-sized. Unlike the homepage's own
     dynamically-named in-page morph (named right before the transition
     starts, on click), a cross-document DESTINATION element must carry its
     name statically at load time — the incoming document's transition is
     captured as the page loads, so there is no "assign it right before"
     option here. Confining the name to desktop is the safe equivalent,
     which also makes the photo morph desktop-only by design, consistent
     with the scroll-reveal above already being desktop-only. This
     breakpoint MUST match the script's own `matchMedia('(min-width: 768px)')`
     desktop gate exactly. Do NOT add this name to the base `.detail-hero__img`
     rule above, and do NOT add it inside the `@media (max-width: 767px)`
     block below. */
  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .detail-hero__img {
      view-transition-name: hero-photo;
    }

    /* quick-260803-bvu (Item 4): CONFIRMED live (getBoundingClientRect
       sampling) that navigating away from an édition detail page AFTER
       scrolling down to read the reveal panel — the whole point of the
       reveal, and completely ordinary visitor behavior — leaves
       `.detail-hero__photo` shrunk toward the reveal's 55%-width settled
       state (measured: 960px vs the full 1280px) at the exact moment the
       outgoing page's View Transition snapshot is captured, while the
       freshly-loaded incoming édition page always starts full-bleed
       (measured: 1280px, scrollY 0). Two documents sharing the same
       `hero-photo` name with that large a size delta forces the browser
       to MORPH between the two box sizes as part of the crossfade —
       exactly the visible shake/jump the client reported. Item 7 briefly
       made the two photos' fitted content diverge more than a uniform crop
       did, but that divergence was reverted by quick-260803-jwl at the
       owner's explicit request, so this size-delta morph is now the SOLE
       cause of the shake — and this suppression is required on its own
       merits regardless of that other change. The fix: stop naming the
       édition hero for view transitions, so an édition-to-édition
       navigation falls back to the site-wide root crossfade
       (`@view-transition { navigation: auto }` in BaseLayout.astro) —
       consistent with the caution already documented in the comment above
       about always-on names on 100svh-sized elements. Galleries never
       carry `.detail-hero--edition` and keep their own shared name
       (proof: tests/e2e/gallery.spec.ts's desktop-present/mobile-absent
       assertions, unchanged). */
    .detail-hero--edition .detail-hero__img {
      view-transition-name: none;
    }
  }

  .detail-hero__scrim {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: linear-gradient(to top, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65) 0%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.15) 45%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0) 70%);
    pointer-events: none;
    opacity: 1;
  }

  /* Item 1 (quick-260725-tqs): sized/positioned/cased to read continuously
     with the homepage carousel's own visible title (.home-hero__title:
     18px, left var(--space-md), ALL CAPS via .toUpperCase()) — this is the
     VISIBLE-on-arrival title (the reveal-title below stays the deliberate
     72px scrolled-down state, untouched).

     DESIGN DECISION: a shared cross-document view-transition-name on the
     two title elements was considered and deliberately NOT added.
     `@view-transition { navigation: auto }` is already enabled site-wide
     (BaseLayout.astro), so a root crossfade already runs on every
     homepage -> gallery-detail navigation; once this overlay title matches
     the homepage title's end-state (size/position/casing), it crossfades
     IN PLACE and reads as the same element — the "reads as continuous, not
     a hard jump" outcome the feedback asked for. A dedicated shared name
     would introduce a new cross-document view-transition group on an
     element inside the 100svh-sized .detail-hero__pin — the same
     always-on-name-on-a-100svh-element risk class (HOME-06/D-10/D-12) that
     forced .detail-hero__img's own name to be desktop-gated — for only
     marginal benefit over an already-continuous in-place crossfade. */
  .detail-hero__overlay-title {
    position: absolute;
    left: var(--space-md);
    bottom: var(--space-xl);
    right: var(--space-xl);
    z-index: 3;
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 18px;
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    line-height: 1.2;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #FFFFFF;
    opacity: 1;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .detail-hero__expand-icon {
    position: absolute;
    bottom: var(--space-lg);
    right: var(--space-lg);
    z-index: 3;
    color: #FFFFFF;
